<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sebele I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1911</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sebele I was chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana people</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, from 1892 to 1911, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sechele-i">Sechele I</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molepolole">Molepolole</a>. In 1895 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khama-iii">Khama III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bathoen-i">Bathoen I</a> to resist the transfer of the Bechuanaland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>, and the chiefs persuaded the British government to keep the protectorate under the Crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sebele-i.jpg" alt="Sebele I" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Theodor Fritsch (1838–1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebele_I_by_Fritsch_1865_better_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
